  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates how the amount and quality of neoantigens in tumour cells shape the clonal makeup that is ultimately observed in a tumour. On the left, three tumour-cell types are shown: one with many low-quality neoantigens, one with only a few high-quality neoantigens, and one with an intermediate neoantigen burden. Small nearby T cells suggest immune recognition differs across these states.\n\nThe right side shows the resulting tumour mixture after selection. In the top scenario, cells carrying a few high-quality neoantigens appear to dominate the observed tumour population, while the intermediate-burden cells remain as a smaller group. The cell type with many low-quality neoantigens seems to contribute little or not at all, implying these numerous weak neoantigens do not strongly determine the surviving tumour composition.\n\nIn the lower scenario, the pattern is different: cells with many low-quality neoantigens now make up most of the observed tumour mixture, whereas the intermediate-burden cells remain minor. Overall, the figure conveys that tumour evolution is not driven simply by neoantigen number. Instead, immune selection depends on neoantigen quality, with different neoantigen landscapes leading to different dominant tumour clones.",
